Found the issue! Look at line 859-860:
```javascript
group.visible = false;
this.mesh = group;
this.scene?.add(group);
```

The weapon mesh is created with `visible = false`! And then it's added to `scene`, NOT to the player's weaponSocket. This is the bug.

Let me check if there's any code that sets it to visible. Looking back at the Player.js code at lines 519-523:
```javascript
if (this.currentWeapon) this.currentWeapon.setVisible(false);
// ...
if (this.currentWeapon) this.currentWeapon.setVisible(true);
```

So the weapon IS being set to visible. But the weapon mesh is in the scene, NOT attached to the player! Let me check if there's code that re-parents it to the socket.

Actually, wait. The weapon is added to `scene`, and then `updateThirdPersonWeapon()` copies the weapon mesh's position/quaternion to match the socket's world position/rotation. But this means the weapon is a child of `scene`, not of the player.

The issue might be that the weapon is being positioned correctly but is somehow not visible. Let me check if there's an issue with the socket world position calculation.

Actually, looking more carefully at `updateThirdPersonWeapon()`:
```javascript
updateThirdPersonWeapon() {
    const socket = this.mesh?.userData?.weaponSocket;
    if (!socket || !this.currentWeapon?.mesh) return;
    const worldPos = this._tmpSocketPos;
    const worldQuat = this._tmpSocketQuat;
    socket.getWorldPosition(worldPos);
    socket.getWorldQuaternion(worldQuat);
    // ...
    this.currentWeapon.mesh.position.copy(worldPos);
    this.currentWeapon.mesh.quaternion.copy(worldQuat);


}
```

This code is copying the socket's world position AND rotation (quaternion) to the weapon. But since the weapon is a child of `scene` (not the player), the world position is also the local position. The problem is that the socket's world quaternion is being copied directly to the weapon, when it should only copy the position and apply a local rotation instead. Since the weapon is a world-space child of scene, applying the socket's world rotation will rotate the weapon in world space rather than keeping it attached to the player's arm.

The rotation should either be reset to identity so the weapon maintains its local orientation within the socket, or the socket's local quaternion should be used instead.

