I see! In line 64-67, there's a `pointerdown` event listener that calls `this.lock()` when the user clicks on the canvas:

```javascript
this._onPointerDown = () => {
    this.domElement.focus?.({ preventScroll: true });
    if (!this.isLocked) this.lock();
};
this.domElement.addEventListener('pointerdown', this._onPointerDown);
```

So when the user clicks on the canvas, `lock()` is called, which requests pointer lock. If successful, `isLocked` becomes `true`, and `isFirstPerson` becomes `true`, showing the viewWeapon.

But what if the user doesn't click on the canvas? Or what if the click fails to activate pointer lock? Then `isFirstPerson` remains `false`, and `fpArms.visible` is `false`, hiding the viewWeapon.

This is the critical issue! The viewWeapon is only visible when pointer lock is active. But the user might not click on the canvas, or the click might fail.

The fix should be: always show the viewWeapon when `isFirstPerson = true`, regardless of whether pointer lock is active. Currently, `fpArms.visible` is controlled by `isFirstPerson`, which depends on `controls.isLocked`.

