const isFirstPersonRaw = (controls && controls.isLocked) || this.input.isMobile;
// Stabilize: flip ONLY when pointer-lock activates (prevents flicker on exit)
// Once in first-person (pointer-lock active), stay in first-person even on brief lock loss
if (isFirstPersonRaw && !this._stableFirstPerson) {
    this._stableFirstPerson = true;
}
const isFirstPerson = this._stableFirstPerson;
```

So the logic is:
1. `_stableFirstPerson` starts as `true` in the constructor
2. In the UPDATE loop, if `isFirstPersonRaw` is `true` AND `_stableFirstPerson` is `false`, set it to `true`
3. Otherwise, `_stableFirstPerson` stays at its current value

Since `_stableFirstPerson` starts as `true`, it stays `true` forever (unless something sets it to `false`). This means `isFirstPerson` is always `true`, so `fpArms.visible` is always `true`, and the weapon is always visible.

